        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 542,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 504,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70446,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70447,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testis; almost always unilateral</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70448,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The patient age range is 12&ndash;87&nbsp;years {8406422; 2212065; 31236304; 32719989}. Patients typically present with a testicular mass, which may rarely be associated with gynaecomastia {31236304; 32719989}. The most common site of metastasis is within retroperitoneal lymph nodes; tumours may also (rarely) involve pelvic lymph nodes, liver, lung, and bone {31236304}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70449,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adult granulosa cell tumours are rare, accounting for &lt;&nbsp;0.5% of all sex cord stromal tumours. There are no known epidemiological associations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70450,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70451,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Acquired <em>FOXL2</em> mutations have been identified less frequently in these tumours than in their ovarian counterparts {21293260; 22742556}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70452,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumour size typically ranges from 30&nbsp;to 50&nbsp;mm {31236304; 32719989}. The tumours are well circumscribed and solid, although cysts may be seen, and the cut surface is lobulated and tan-white to yellowish-tan.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70453,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours often have a nodular growth pattern, with the neoplastic cells within the tumour nodules growing diffusely. Microfollicles (Call&ndash;Exner bodies), trabeculae, nests, cords, gyriform structures, or spindled morphology can be seen. The associated stroma is typically inconspicuous but when present may be fibrous or oedematous. The cells have scant cytoplasm and elongated nuclei, often with nuclear grooves; luteinization of the cells and a few mitotic figures may be seen.</p>\r\n<p>The tumour cells may be positive for SF1, vimentin, inhibin, calretinin, SMA, S100, ER, PR, CD56, and cytokeratins (weak or dot-like); EMA, desmin, and HMB45 are negative {8406422; 2212065; 16532229; 24705318}. FOXL2 shows consistent staining in the few cases in which this immunostain has been performed {21293260}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70454,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70455,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of <em>FOXL2</em> mutations may be helpful {21293260; 22742556}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70456,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> composed of small ovoid cells with elongated, often grooved nuclei and scant cytoplasm; various architectural patterns exist.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunohistochemistry for SF1, inhibin, or calretinin may be helpful in selected cases; reticulin may be helpful; FOXL2 immunoreactivity is frequent, but testicular cases do not consistently show somatic <em>FOXL2</em> gene mutation; may show Call&ndash;Exner bodies.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-15T15:55:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70457,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70458,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Given the overall rarity of these tumours and the fact that a small number have demonstrated malignant behaviour, it is difficult to assess features that consistently predict which tumours will metastasize. However, larger tumour size (&gt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;mm) and vascular invasion are the most consistent features across several studies that show significant correlation with malignant behaviour {31236304; 32719989; 10494291; 18304605; 21204721}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:52:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]